 "notes": [
  "Revenue per store per day is revenue over the average stores and the days: Rs 41.8 lakh in FY26. Bigger new stores and same-store growth both move it, so its growth is not the company's like-for-like growth (9.5% for stores two years and older in Q2 FY27, by the company's release).",
  "The average count of stores is worked from the counts at each quarter end, because FY26's openings were uneven (58 of the 85 came in its fourth quarter); halving the year's two ends would overstate it.",
  "Goods sold is purchases plus the change in stock; DMart prints no power line (power and rent sit in other expenses and, under Ind AS 116, in depreciation and finance costs).",
  "The filing does not split interest on leases out of finance costs, so all of it is interest on borrowings and the rate on borrowings that comes out is high; interest on leases is left at nil.",
  "Other income is more than interest on cash: the investments in subsidiaries are in the non-current assets, so the yield on cash that comes out is high."
 ],
